+4.5 - Externally-signed updates
+
+   A zone administrator MAY, as a matter of policy (Section 3), configure
+   a zone such that the server verifies SIG records supplied by the
+   update principal instead of generating them itself.  This policy is
+   OPTIONAL to implement and OPTIONAL to enable per zone; a server or
+   zone not configured for it behaves exactly as specified elsewhere in
+   this document.
+
+   4.5.1 - Applicability
+
+      This policy MAY be enabled only for a zone whose signing key(s)
+      are not held online at the server.  It is intended for deployments
+      in which the party authorized to update the zone (Section 3) also
+      holds the zone's DNSSEC signing key(s), and signs data before it
+      is sent, rather than delegating signing to the server as described
+      in Section 4.3.
+
+   4.5.2 - Transaction authentication
+
+      Section 2 continues to apply: TSIG or SIG(0) MUST be used to
+      authenticate the update message, and the principal so authenticated
+      is checked against policy (Section 3) as usual.  Per the amendment
+      to Section 2 above, the SIG(0) key MAY be a zone key when this
+      policy is in effect.  How a server first associates a principal's
+      key with authorization to update a given zone is a matter of
+      server policy and is outside the scope of this document; [SAZU]
+      defines one such mechanism.
+
+   4.5.3 - Verification of supplied SIG records
+
+      For each RRset added or modified by the update, and for any SOA or
+      NXT/NSEC/NSEC3 record supplied under Section 4.4, an accompanying
+      SIG (RRSIG) record MUST be present and MUST be verified by the
+      server against a DNSKEY the server currently trusts for the zone
+      before the update is applied.  Verification MUST confirm that:
+
+      (a)  the SIG's temporal validity period (inception and expiration)
+           is currently valid;
+
+      (b)  the SIG's algorithm is acceptable under the server's
+           configured policy;
+
+      (c)  the SIG's owner name, type covered, and RDATA correspond
+           exactly to the RRset it accompanies; and
+
+      (d)  the signature itself is cryptographically valid for that
+           RRset under the referenced DNSKEY.
+
+      If any supplied SIG fails verification under (a) through (d), or
+      is absent where required by this section, the server MUST reject
+      the update in its entirety with RCODE REFUSED and MUST NOT apply
+      any part of it.  This is a change from Section 4.1, under which an
+      unverified SIG is ordinarily added rather than checked.
+
+   4.5.4 - Key rollover
+
+      An update MAY include a new DNSKEY record for the zone.  A DNSKEY
+      so added MUST itself be covered by a SIG verified per Section
+      4.5.3 under a DNSKEY already trusted for the zone, except for the
+      zone's first DNSKEY, which is trusted on receipt of the first
+      authenticated update for that zone if and only if it matches a DS
+      record already published for the zone in the parent zone, per the
+      mechanism defined in [SAZU].  A server MUST continue to accept
+      updates authenticated by a previously-trusted DNSKEY until that
+      key is explicitly removed by a verified update, to allow an
+      orderly transition between keys.
 
